    {
      "id": "claim_08920cf1ab914c7593265b9910fc3e04",
      "statement": "get_date_taken resolves the capture date via exifread.process_file with stop_tag \"EXIF DateTimeOriginal\", parses the tag with datetime.strptime using the format '%Y:%m:%d %H:%M:%S', and on any OSError, KeyError (tag missing), or ValueError (malformed date) logs a warning and falls back to datetime.fromtimestamp(os.path.getctime(path)). It never raises for missing or malformed EXIF, so every supported-format file always yields a datetime for the YYYY/MM/DD folder structure.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://sortinfolder.py#L95-L123",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:049c9c823a71185b87c898275f1cf0a560eea6dfeca3586d1129b673a4b9d0ed:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_6bd41242c4354458aef82befc4eddf03",
      "statement": "rotate_image_according_to_exif applies rotation only for EXIF Orientation values 3, 6, and 8 — mapping to img.rotate(180, expand=True), rotate(270, expand=True), and rotate(90, expand=True) respectively — and re-saves the file in place (img.save(image_path)) only in those three cases; all other values (including 1 and None) result in no save.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://sortinfolder.py#L55-L94",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a3da03f57c6fb0c71e5d8b9c0269a41919b2c4f6239a7b55e21777472ed51d31:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_82bf38932923482983baab3df5817b05",
      "statement": "get_exif_orientation reads the raw Orientation tag by calling Pillow's private img._getexif() accessor and scanning PIL.ExifTags.TAGS for the id named 'Orientation'; it returns None when EXIF is absent, and it swallows AttributeError, KeyError, IndexError, and TypeError (logging at debug level and returning None), so it never raises for missing metadata.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://sortinfolder.py#L31-L54",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a85138a8d78bf53505b7d7035b32148b9e88dbfe2dcf9f4bf502f2dde332cd19:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_cb99073b255c4f7faa7e6c35b574b9ee",
      "statement": "rotate_image_according_to_exif treats truncated image data as non-fatal: an OSError from img.load() is logged as 'Image file is truncated' and the function returns without raising; by contrast, a bare OSError from Image.open is re-raised, and UnidentifiedImageError or struct.error are logged and re-raised, so undecodable files count as per-file failures in the sort_images summary.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://sortinfolder.py#L64-L94",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8184244c8a47887f660732203a50519819d02d1ea60cfac30ee33b425cf7c8b5:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_2242ec5d9d954c488e6848210590e07f",
      "statement": "add_relative_path_tag is gated on a .jpg/.jpeg extension check: for JPEGs it loads the EXIF block with piexif.load, appends b\"\\nRelativePath:\" plus the encoded relative path to the existing UserComment (defaulting to empty bytes), and writes it back via piexif.dump and piexif.insert; for any other extension it logs 'File format not supported for tagging' at info level and leaves the file untouched. The relative path itself is computed by process_image as os.path.relpath(os.path.dirname(file_path), basepath).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://sortinfolder.py#L124-L155",
